    "result": "I implemented `compare_versions` in `solution.py` and wrote a 115-test pytest suite in `tests/test_solution.py`. `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all 115 tests. The implementation validates each version against the spec's grammar and raises `ValueError` for anything malformed, including non-string inputs. It then builds a precedence key per version: the core numbers come first, then a release (no prerelease) sorts above any prerelease with the same core, and prerelease identifiers compare numeric-before-alphanumeric with fewer-identifiers-is-lower. `BUILD` metadata is validated but excluded from the key. The tests cover ordering on every spec rule, the invalid-input cases the spec lists (leading zeros, empty identifiers, extra parts, stray characters), valid edge cases, and a sorted semver chain plus antisymmetry and transitivity checks. I did not run coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit checks myself, so those gates are unverified. The functions are small and the tests exercise each branch, so I expect them to pass, but I haven't confirmed it.",
